From de7cd59f5b3389b34c726b980f7eb3d0a88d6516 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Wed, 15 Jul 2026 12:08:42 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 07188/11362] [outer-loop] REQ-ARC-FCP-5699-7: budget was not
 the limiting factor either

Answers the open follow-up from REQ-ARC-FCP-5699-6's control: "whether
a MUCH longer budget lets either router eventually escape level 0."

Added --budget N to the smoke-test CLI, writing to a _budgetN-suffixed
path so a longer run never overwrites the 46-budget artifacts it
needs comparing against.

Ran BOTH SGE and the deterministic baseline at budget=250 (~5.4x)
against all 3 games. All 6 runs (3 games x 2 router modes) again show
real_max_level_observed=0, confirmed against each raw action_log,
with 239-248 real attempts per game -- near-full budget consumption,
not an early stop. SGE took 141-486s/game (real LLM calls), baseline
2.4-5.4s/game (no LLM), a ~5.4x wall-clock scaling matching the
~5.4x action-count increase.

This rules out "insufficient budget" for this harness. Combined with
REQ-ARC-FCP-5699-6's router-independence finding, the two most
obvious explanations for every "0/3 leveled up" result in this
investigation -- SGE underperforms; not enough time -- are both now
ruled out. The one remaining, untested hypothesis: one of the other
deliberately-disabled production features (induction, frame-change
scorer, goal-bias, go-explore archive) is what's actually
load-bearing for a first level-up on g50t/sk48/cd82, independent of
router and budget. Logged as the concrete next fork in
ops/known-issues.md task 6, rather than further router/budget
re-testing on these 3 games.

2 new tests (budget flows through to run_game, spec-declaration).
Spec: REQ-ARC-FCP-5699-7 with its own scenario.

